    "hypotheses": [
      "The inherited setting is a hyperbolic equation equipped with Riemann initial data (printed page 5, Section 1.2.1, opening definition).",
      "The initial state is piecewise constant: q(x,0) = q_l for x < 0 and q(x,0) = q_r for x > 0 (printed page 5, equation (1.11)).",
      "In the extension under discussion, the medium also has a discontinuity at x = 0, coinciding with the initial-data discontinuity (printed page 8, Section 1.4, 'at x = 0 as well as a discontinuity in the initial data')."
    ],
    "conclusions": [
      "The Riemann-problem setup extends to an interface at which both the medium and the initial state are discontinuous, retaining the inherited left and right constant initial states on the two open half-lines (printed page 8, selected extension sentence, together with printed page 5, equation (1.11))."
    ],
    "definitions_and_conventions": [
      "A Riemann problem is the hyperbolic equation together with the specified special initial data, rather than the initial data alone (printed page 5, Section 1.2.1, opening sentence).",
      "The subscripts l and r identify the states on x < 0 and x > 0, respectively (printed page 5, equation (1.11)).",
      "Equation (1.11) assigns no value at x = 0 (printed page 5, the two strict inequalities in the displayed cases).",
      "The origin is a chosen interface location: the original definition says 'at some point, say x = 0' (printed page 5, paragraph preceding equation (1.11)).",
      "A discontinuity in the medium refers to material heterogeneity, distinct from a discontinuity in the state data (printed page 7, discussion of material-parameter jumps; printed page 8, selected sentence)."
    ],
